I understand you want a specific answer. Let me ask you to examine this critical section:

```java
// Read search ID
int searchId = sc.nextInt();

if (searchId <= 0 || !employees.containsKey(searchId)) {
    System.out.println("Employee ID not found");
    return;
}

// Display searched employee salary
System.out.println(employees.get(searchId));

// Read update ID
int updateId = sc.nextInt();

if (updateId <= 0 || !employees.containsKey(updateId)) {
    System.out.println("Employee ID not found");
    return;
}

// Read new salary
double newSalary = sc.nextDouble();

if (newSalary <= 0) {
    System.out.println("Invalid salary");
    return;
}

// Update the correct employee
employees.put(updateId, newSalary);

// Display records after update
displayRecords(employees);

// Read employee ID to remove
int removeId = sc.nextInt();

if (removeId <= 0 || !employees.containsKey(removeId)) {
    System.out.println("Employee ID not found");
    return;
}

// Remove the correct employee
employees.remove(removeId);

// Display records after removal
displayRecords(employees);
```

What happens if the search ID is valid, but then you proceed to read the update ID from the scanner? Is there a possibility that the scanner position or the way you're reading the input is causing issues with the subsequent operations?

The key insight is: **what if the issue is not in your logic, but in how you're consuming input from the Scanner?**
